      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, quick question. i'm trying to clean up a schema we've been using for a while. we're currently in 2NF but there's a lot of redundant data popping up in some of the tables. can you walk me through the steps to get us to 3NF? specifically how to handle transitive dependencies?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! To transition from 2NF to 3NF and eliminate redundant data, you'll need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify all the candidate keys in your 2NF tables.\n2. Examine each table and identify any columns that depend on any non-primary key attributes. This includes columns that have transitive dependencies.\n3. Create new tables for each transitive dependency and move the affected rows from the original table to the new tables.\n4. Update the remaining original tables to reference the new tables by using their primary keys.\n5. Ensure that the new tables achieve 2NF, as they are separate entities but still need to avoid redundant data.\n6. Re-assess any remaining transitive dependencies and repeat the process until all tables are in 3NF.\n\nRemember to keep your changes manageable and consistent; it's often helpful to automate parts of this process using scripts if needed. Good luck!",
      "source": "target",
