      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that makes sense. can you give me a concrete example though? like if i have an 'employees' table with employee_id, name, department_id, and department_name, how does that specifically show a transitive dependency and how would the 3NF version look like? i want to make sure i dont mess up the foreign keys when i split them.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In your 'employees' table, let's take the 'department_id' and 'department_name' attributes. There is a transitive dependency because the 'department_name' value depends on the 'department_id' (and not directly on the 'employee_id'), creating redundancy. To move into 3NF:\n\n1. Create a new 'departments' table with two columns: 'department_id' (as a primary key, unique department identifier) and 'department_name' (the department's name).\n\n2. Insert the original department names into the 'departments' table.\n\n3. Update the 'employees' table:\n   - Delete 'department_id' and 'department_name' columns.\n   - Add a new column, 'department_id', and remove 'name' (if it was present).\n\n4. Add a foreign key constraint in the 'employees' table for 'department_id' that references the primary key of the 'departments' table.\n\nThe modified 'employees' table will only have the 'employee_id', the column representing the employee's name, and the 'department_id'. It's also essential to maintain referential integrity between 'employees' and 'departments' tables when performing these operations.\n\nBy doing this, all information about departments",
      "source": "target",
